I implemented `createParser(options)` in `src/csv.js`, and `node --test` passes (7/7). `parse` and `src/rows.js` didn't need changes; `toObjects` already matches its documented behaviour.

The parser reads the input one character at a time and keeps its position between pushes, so a chunk can end anywhere: inside `""`, between CR and LF, or in the middle of a quoted field. Each record is returned as soon as its LF or CR arrives. A CR is enough, and if the next chunk starts with LF, that LF is skipped and the line number isn't counted twice.

- **Errors and lifecycle:** delimiter, `push`, `end` and BOM handling follow the JSDoc. A bad delimiter throws `RangeError` when the parser is created. A non-string chunk throws `TypeError`. Any push after `end`, even `push('')`, throws `Error`. A second `end()` returns `[]`. Empty chunks are ignored and don't stop a BOM from being stripped later.
- **Unfinished record:** `end()` returns a final record only if one has started (for example `a,` or `""`). Input that finishes on a record separator returns nothing more.
- **Line numbers:** CR and LF inside quoted fields are counted too, and CRLF counts as one line. Text after a closing quote (spaces included) throws `SyntaxError` with `line N`, and so does an unterminated quote at `end()`.

The existing tests only cover single-chunk input, so I also ran a temporary script (now deleted) that fed each test input split at every pair of positions, with an empty chunk added in. It checked:
- CRLF, lone CR and doubled quotes split across chunks;
- BOM handling and surrogate pairs;
- custom and invalid delimiters;
- the error line numbers;
- each record being returned straight away;
- header mode: duplicate and `__proto__` names, padding, an empty file and a header-only file.

Everything passed.
